--TEST--
`Core\Env::all` is name-ordered and agrees with `get` on every name it holds — spec § 15, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`
--ENV--
NVS_CASE_C=third
NVS_CASE_A=first
NVS_CASE_B=second
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `all` sweeps the whole environment, so a case can only pin the part it set
// itself — everything else belongs to the machine this runs on.

var $all = Core\Env::all();

// **Name order, whatever order the environment was built in.** The section
// above writes C, A, B; the sweep answers A, B, C on every platform, which is
// what makes an answer this wide printable at all. The accumulator is
// `tainted` because each value is: `rule:security/tainted-qualifier` says the environment is
// outside the program's own text, so nothing here launders and the qualifier
// reaches the end of the concatenation.
tainted string $ours = "";
foreach ($all as tainted string $name => tainted string $value) {
    if (Core\Str::startsWith($name, "NVS_CASE_")) {
        $ours = $ours . $name . "=" . $value . ";";
    }
}
echo $ours, "\n";

// **Agreement**, asserted by counting rather than read off a line: a member
// that answered plausibly for one name and drifted on another still fails
// here. `all` and `get` are two readings of one environment, so every name in
// the first has to answer the same way through the second.
array<string> $names = ["NVS_CASE_A", "NVS_CASE_B", "NVS_CASE_C"];
int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($names as string $wanted) {
    if (Core\Arr::hasKey($all, $wanted) && $all[$wanted] == Core\Env::get($wanted)) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}
echo "agreed: ", $agreed as string, " of ", Core\Arr::count($names) as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
NVS_CASE_A=first;NVS_CASE_B=second;NVS_CASE_C=third;
agreed: 3 of 3
